# What the coverage report (#139) does not count, and why.
#
# The report lists every node the evaluator never marked. Some nodes are not
# constructs that run, and this file is where that gap is written down instead
# of being hidden in a ratio (#134). One kind per line:
#
#   node|subtree <kind> <reason>
#
# `node` exempts the node alone; `subtree` exempts it and everything under it.
# The kind is spelled as `ludo ast` prints it. A kind missing from this list is
# counted, so a new AST kind shows up in the report until someone gives it a
# line here, with its reason.

# Type expressions. The names inside them resolve when the program is checked,
# and nothing in them runs.
subtree named-type     a type expression: resolved by the checker, never evaluated
subtree optional-type  a type expression: resolved by the checker, never evaluated
subtree view-type      a type expression: resolved by the checker, never evaluated
subtree mutable-type   a type expression: resolved by the checker, never evaluated
subtree fn-type        a type expression: resolved by the checker, never evaluated
subtree array-type     a type expression: its length is a constant the checker folds
subtree pointer-type   a type expression: resolved by the checker, never evaluated
subtree fallible-type  a type expression: resolved by the checker, never evaluated
subtree generic-params a declaration's type parameters: instantiated by the checker (ch2 5.1)

# Declarations the checker discharges whole. Nothing in them is code.
subtree use        names another module; the module mapping resolves it (ch4)
subtree attribute  #align is type-level layout, read by the checker (ch3 8.3)
subtree interface  signatures only, no bodies (ch2 6.7.2)
subtree impl       a satisfaction assertion with no body (ch2 6.7.3); the fns that discharge it are counted

# Declaration structure. These carry names and shape; what runs is below them.
node module      the file itself: its declarations are what load and run
node root        the `$` a path starts from: the checker resolves it, the access on it runs
node type-decl   declares a type; only its fields' defaults run (ch1 5.3)
node struct      a type's shape; only its fields' defaults run (ch1 5.3)
node field       a field's name and type; its default, if any, is counted
node enum        a type's shape: a variant runs when an expression names it
node variant     a variant's declaration; constructing one is the expression that names it
node distinct    a type's shape, with no runtime part
node params      a parameter list; each param is marked when a call binds it
node returns     a declared result type, which the checker reads
